{"author":{"id":"ec747d12c042242d4b39b77b9587ceaf8e1b9b6ee25094cf6129b703c40b5b9c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1775726199,"nonce":"KFun7fSQAI0VFvZwMWaO2f8H1Sg=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs78MDKU","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/26","origin":"github"},"title":"Implementation of adaptive LR schedulers and Weight schedulers","message":"**Overview**\nStandard static training configurations (fixed learning rate, fixed loss weights) are insufficient for PINN problems due to gradient imbalances between competing loss terms To address this, three adaptive training strategies have been implemented in `adaptive.py`, each sourced from a dedicated research paper.\n\n**What is done**\n1. DLRS — Dynamic Learning Rate Scheduler\n[Improving Neural Network Training using Dynamic Learning Rate Schedule for PINNs and Image Classification](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.21749)\n- Dynamically adjusts the global learning rate each epoch based on the normalised loss slope computed over a rolling window of batches decreasing LR on divergence/stagnation, increasing it on convergence.\n\n2. ReLoBRaLo — Relative Loss Balancing with Random Lookback\n[Multi-Objective Loss Balancing for Physics-Informed Deep Learning]\n(https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0045782525001860)\n- Adaptively scales each loss term's weight using a softmax of relative loss improvements, with a random lookback mechanism that occasionally resets the reference to epoch 0 to prevent forgetting early progress.\n\n3. lbPINNs — Self-Adaptive Loss Balanced PINNs\n[Self-adaptive loss balanced Physics-informed neural networks - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S092523122200546X)\n- Learns a log-variance parameter per loss term, jointly optimised with network weights, so that harder terms are automatically up-weighted while a regulariser prevents any term from being ignored entirely.\n\n**Why Was This Done**\nEvaluate whether adaptive LR control (DLRS) or adaptive loss weighting (ReLoBRaLo, lbPINNs) improves solution accuracy \n\n**Files Changed**\n `adaptive.py` — all three schedulers implemented here","files":null}]}